<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miyoshi Nagayoshi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1522">1522</a>–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Miyoshi Chōkei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miyoshi Nagayoshi was a Japanese warlord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> who dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and the home provinces in the 1550s. A retainer of the Hosokawa deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a>, he turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-harumoto">Hosokawa Harumoto</a> in 1549, defeated him at Eguchi and drove the shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiteru">Ashikaga Yoshiteru</a> from the capital. His regime is sometimes seen as the first unifying power in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miyoshi-nagayoshi.jpg" alt="Miyoshi Nagayoshi" width="320" height="698" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 投稿者がファイル作成 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B8%89%E5%A5%BD%E9%95%B7%E6%85%B6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nagayoshi was born in 1522, the eldest son of Miyoshi Motonaga. In 1536 he became a commander under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-harumoto">Hosokawa Harumoto</a> and fought the rebels of Hosokawa Harukuni and the Hongan-ji militants at Nakajima in Settsu. In 1539 he led his army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> for the first time, and that year he made Koshimizu Castle in Settsu his base. Earlier heads of the Miyoshi had ruled from Awa on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> and fallen back there in times of trouble; Nagayoshi never returned to Awa and became deputy governor of Settsu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Collecting taxes in Settsu on his own authority, he fell out with Harumoto, and his power grew after the fall of Kizawa Nagamasa in 1542. He then rebelled against Harumoto in alliance with Hosokawa Ujitsuna and Yusa Naganori, defeated Harumoto's allies and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, while the shoguns Ashikaga Yoshiharu and Yoshiteru fled to Omi. The capture of Nagao Castle, the last stronghold of Yoshiteru's forces, in late 1550 brought down the Hosokawa government and began what historians call the Miyoshi government.</p><p>Yoshiteru tried to have him assassinated in 1551, and that July Nagayoshi's army of 40,000 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsunaga-hisahide">Matsunaga Hisahide</a> fought Harumoto's men at Shokoku-ji in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. A peace of 1552 brought Yoshiteru back as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, but real power stayed with Nagayoshi; after a new clash in 1553 Yoshiteru spent five years in seclusion at Kutsuki. By 1561 Nagayoshi's influence reached ten provinces, and that year he issued a privilege for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary Vilela. From the death of his brother Sogō Kazumasa in 1561 his fortunes declined: his brother Miyoshi Saneyasu was killed at Kumeda in 1562, though Nagayoshi won the Battle of Kyōkōji that May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1563 he lost his only son, Miyoshi Yoshioki, and adopted his nephew Yoshitsugu. In 1564 he had his brother Atagi Fuyuyasu put to death, believing, according to the chronicle Ashikaga Kiseki, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsunaga-hisahide">Matsunaga Hisahide</a>'s lie that Fuyuyasu meant to seize the clan's leadership. He died of illness that year, aged 42. From the mid-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> writers cast him as a weak backdrop to the villainous Hisahide, and Rai San'yō called him old, sick and ignorant. Modern historians such as Amano Tadayuki and Akira Imatani have revised this, Amano seeing his government as the forerunner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>'s unification, while Thomas D. Conlan called him the brutal conqueror of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miyoshi Nagayoshi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1549 <b>Miyoshi Nagayoshi</b> turned against his master Hosokawa Harumoto, defeated his forces at Eguchi and took control of Kyoto, driving the shogun into exile.</blockquote><a href="/state/miyoshi-clan-domain">Miyoshi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shibō Castle. Died in Iimoriyama Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.73,135.65&amp;year=1549" aria-label="Iimoriyama Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Miyoshi Nagayoshi"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4002 4533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4002 4533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.73,135.65&amp;year=1549">What was here in 1549? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
